  "notes": "Def Jam's Caribbean-inflected radio-R&B, **claimed here for 2005–2010 only** on the research's own instruction; the 2011+ EDM-pop era belongs to a pop lane and is not modelled. Tempo is \"87–100 straight (backbeat 2&4) plus a 122–130 club tail from 2007\", and the R&B band is the base with the mode at 87 — the sourced tempo of the signature record, in B♭ minor with a bridge lift to G♭ major. The identifying drum parameter is unusual and the research names it: the record is built on \"GarageBand's Vintage Funk Kit 03\" loop, chosen for its \"'walloping' hi-hat as the hook element\". So the hat lane is the loudest thing in this model — 0.78 fill density with a main-velocity band running to 1.0, the highest ceiling in this section, plus a ramped roll at 0.3 per bar — over a plain kick on 1 and the 8th before 3 and a snare flat on 2 and 4 pushed 2–6 ms with a loose 0.72 quantize, which is what a sampled funk loop does. The stadium snare reverb has no schema field and is recorded here. Harmony is \"two- to four-chord minor vamps with synth pads and a hi-hat-led groove\". \"Melody built from short repeated cells with a hard consonant attack\" is the staccato articulation, `twoBarCellRepeat` and a riff-dominant contour; \"harmony stacks are dense and gang-vocal-like on the hook\" is a counter at −12 led by `octave_echo` at a 0.5 density. The low end is \"a **distorted, overdriven bassline** rather than a clean 808 — saturation is the signature\", so it is a bass part and there is no `drums.bass808` block. ⚠ Every velocity and density number is INFERRED. ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `Bbm`, `Gb`, and the remaining values `Fm`, `Cm`, together with all 4 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry.",
